Give the samplers a defined result for an infinite, a NaN or a very large logit
Fold-into: upstream/sampling

The defects. tests/fuzz/core/run.sh test replays the reproducer of each one.

- The bucket sort converts a float outside the range of int to int.

  llama_token_data_array_partial_sort puts each logit in one of 128 buckets with
  int(bucket_scale * logit + bucket_inter), and clamps the int after the conversion. For a logit
  above 3.3e8 in magnitude, or an Inf, the conversion is undefined, and x86 gives INT_MIN. The
  highest logits then go to the lowest bucket. top-k with more than 128 candidates and top-p on
  more than 1024 candidates use this sort, thus they keep the wrong tokens.

  The change: The index is clamped in float before the conversion. A NaN goes to the lowest bucket.

  The reproducer: tests/fuzz/core/regress/fuzz_sampler--sampler-bucket-cast.

- The dist sampler selects no candidate when a logit is +Inf, all logits are -Inf, or a logit is NaN.

  dist computes exp(l - max_l). For l = max_l = +Inf or -Inf, the value is NaN, and a NaN logit
  makes a NaN too. The sum is then NaN, no candidate reaches the target, and dist selects the last
  candidate (release) or fails assert(found) (debug). A sorted array also takes max_l from the first
  candidate, which can be a NaN.

  The change: max_l comes from a loop that a NaN never wins. When max_l is infinite, each candidate
  with the logit max_l gets an equal share of the probability. A NaN logit gets the probability 0.
  For finite logits, the probabilities are the same as those of the unpatched code.

  The reproducer: tests/fuzz/core/regress/fuzz_sampler--dist-inf.

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 with the patches of patches/series above this one.

